g TestRepo startup.\n     - Also consider `cargo test --test integration test_utils_unit` if relevant.\n   - Check compile errors from new PATH sanitization (`join(&separator.to_string())` lifetime/type ok? likely okay but verify).\n\n2. Implement hook stdin timeout:\n   - Add a bounded read for `--hook-input stdin` in `src/commands/git_ai_handlers.rs`.\n   - Requirement: if stdin isn’t closed by hook host, process should exit after small timeout rather than remain forever.\n   - Should avoid adding heavy deps. Possible approaches:\n     - Spawn a thread to read stdin into buffer and use `std::sync::mpsc::recv_timeout`.\n     - Timeout from env var `GIT_AI_HOOK_STDIN_TIMEOUT_MS`, default maybe 5000ms.\n     - If timeout, log/diagnostic event and exit 0 (hooks shouldn’t block user workflow).\n   - Need be careful: blocking stdin read thread may remain until process exits, but if main exits with `std::process::exit(0)`, process terminates.\n   - Add tests if possible. Unit test may be hard because it reads real stdin; at minimum add helper function for timeout parsing and maybe test that. Better refactor read logic into function accepting `Read` and timeout? But real timeout with arbitrary `Read` can be hard cross-platform.\n\n3. Implement stale active runtime fallback:\n   - In `DaemonConfig::from_default_paths` / `active_runtime_config`, if `active-runtime.json` points to replacement runtime but that runtime is not healthy and lock is not held, remove `active-runtime.json` and return default config.\n   - Must not do expensive process scans.\n   - Need avoid dependency loop: `daemon_is_up` is in `src/commands/daemon.rs`, not accessible from `src/daemon.rs`? There are lower-level functions in `src/daemon.rs`: `local_socket_connects_with_timeout` etc. Could implement a lightweight `config_sockets_connect` method in `src/daemon.rs` or keep fallback in `src/commands/daemon.rs` by adding a `resolve_healthy_daemon_config` wrapper. But many code paths call `DaemonConfig::from_env_or_default_paths` directly, including `handle_checkpoint`; so best in `src/daemon.rs`.\n   - Windows pipe paths don’t exist as files, so do not use `.exists()` for named pipes. Use `local_socket_connects_with_timeout` if available at that point. Function defined later in same module, can call.\n   - Active runtime is stale if control and trace sockets cannot connect within small timeout AND lock is free. If lock is held, keep active config (startup in progress/block).\n   - If stale, remove `active-runtime.json` best-effort.\n   - Add unit tests if possible using temp dirs and fake metadata where lock is free.\n\n4. Possibly improve installer/daemon cleanup documentation:\n   - Update `docs/design-doc/git-ai看板方案.md`:\n     - State 2.29 was incomplete.\n     - Add new Phase row 2.31 or update 2.29/2.30/2.31:\n       - “运行时健康收敛：阻断测试 PATH 污染、hook stdin 超时、active runtime stale fallback”\n     - Mention performance: no per-command full process scan; cleanup only at test env setup, hook timeout, daemon start/restart/install.\n   - If code changes include new tests, document verification commands.\n\n5. Consider adding Windows installer script checks:\n   - If you update installer further, add to `tests/windows_script_checks.rs`.\n   - But primary root cause now seems not installer script itself; it is runtime/test contamination and hanging hook stdin.\n\n**Already Established Conclusion To Communicate**\n- “2.29 has a design gap.”\n- It solves “can install over a busy exe,” but not “why exe is busy / why many processes exist / why upload start is delayed.”\n- Thorough solution should be layered:\n  1. Prevent orphan hook processes (`--hook-input stdin` timeout).\n  2. Prevent stale active runtime routing (healthy runtime check + fallback).\n  3. Prevent test PATH pollution on Windows (already started).\n  4. Keep installer retirement as last-resort safety, not normal control plane.\n\n"
